WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.112
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.112 --> 00:00:06.519
<v Examiner>Identify the six pou of the Registered Nurse Standards of Competence,

3
00:00:06.519 --> 00:00:11.373
<v Examiner>and explain the scope of practice of a B N 2 student nurse, with an example.

4
00:00:11.823 --> 00:00:12.648
The six pou.

5
00:00:12.648 --> 00:00:13.886
One, Māori health.

6
00:00:13.886 --> 00:00:16.636
Two, cultural safety, kawa whakaruruhau.

7
00:00:16.636 --> 00:00:19.387
Three, whanaungatanga and communication.

8
00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:23.375
Four, pūkengatanga and evidence-informed nursing practice.

9
00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:26.332
Five, manaakitanga and people-centred care.

10
00:00:26.332 --> 00:00:28.738
Six, rangatiratanga and leadership.

11
00:00:28.738 --> 00:00:34.514
Each pou comes with descriptors: examples of what competence looks like in practice.

12
00:00:34.514 --> 00:00:38.640
The words in them: whanaungatanga is building relationships,

13
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.004
manaakitanga is caring and respect, rangatiratanga is leadership and advocacy,

14
00:00:44.004 --> 00:00:46.754
and pūkengatanga is skill and expertise.

15
00:00:47.204 --> 00:00:48.074
Student scope.

16
00:00:48.074 --> 00:00:51.556
As a second-year student you are not a registered nurse.

17
00:00:51.556 --> 00:00:54.975
You work under the direction and supervision of an R N,

18
00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:59.265
and you only do what you've been taught and assessed as competent in.

19
00:00:59.265 --> 00:01:02.497
Anything outside that, you decline and tell the R N.

20
00:01:02.497 --> 00:01:07.284
Both of the lecturers' practice quiz questions on scope have the same answer.

21
00:01:07.284 --> 00:01:11.636
A doctor gives you a telephone order: decline, it's not in your scope,

22
00:01:11.636 --> 00:01:13.066
and hand it to the R N.

23
00:01:13.066 --> 00:01:18.226
Your preceptor asks you to go and give an overdue paracetamol on your own: decline,

24
00:01:18.226 --> 00:01:19.469
for the same reason.

25
00:01:19.469 --> 00:01:23.510
Lisa's multi-choice tip fits here too: answer as a student nurse,

26
00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.997
so tell the R N can be the right answer.

27
00:01:26.447 --> 00:01:31.338
If the question is on roles of nurses instead: nurses work across aged care,

28
00:01:31.338 --> 00:01:36.616
with multimorbidity, independence and palliative care; primary and community care,

29
00:01:36.616 --> 00:01:42.344
across the whole lifespan, with a focus on prevention; acute care, with rapid assessment;

30
00:01:42.344 --> 00:01:46.270
mental health, with recovery and resilience; and informatics,

31
00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:49.295
using digital systems and data to support care.

32
00:01:49.295 --> 00:01:53.285
Continuum of care means services that cover the whole journey,

33
00:01:53.285 --> 00:01:58.370
from prevention to acute care, rehabilitation and community care, without gaps.

34
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.574
Six pou: Māori health; cultural safety; whanaungatanga and communication;

35
00:02:03.574 --> 00:02:07.742
pūkengatanga and evidence; manaakitanga and people-centred care;

36
00:02:07.742 --> 00:02:09.696
rangatiratanga and leadership.

37
00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:15.231
Student scope: under an R N's supervision, only what you've been taught and assessed.

38
00:02:15.231 --> 00:02:16.924
Decline a telephone order.

39
00:02:16.924 --> 00:02:18.617
Decline giving meds alone.
